@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why every credential decision is a pure function, and what the one writer is allowed to do
' covers: internal/credentials/credentials.go
' covers: internal/credentials/hints.go
'
' ── PURE OVER (manifest, lookup, homeRoot) ─────────────────────────────────
' This package decides WHAT a run authenticates with and writes almost nothing.
' Every function is pure over those three inputs: no run state, no cobra, no
' docker, no env read of its own. `lookup` is the single credential read in a run
' and it is passed IN, so a test can answer it and a caller can point it at a
' workspace .env instead of the process environment.
'
' That purity is why the package could be lifted out of a 3505-line main.go
' without a single plan golden moving. It is also what makes the decisions
' testable at all: AuthSuppressor, BrokerProviders and PersistedLogin each encode
' a rule that was previously an `if` buried in a 600-line function.
'
' ── THE RULES WORTH KNOWING ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' AuthSuppressor · a provider with TWO ways to authenticate gets only the one the
'   operator chose. Handing both to a backend put an API key and a subscription
'   token in the same store, and the proxy picked the key — so a subscription run
'   billed per token and the auth row the operator answered was overruled
'   somewhere they could not see.
' PersistedLogin · a completed login in the proveo home IS a credential.
'   MissingEnv only reads env vars, so a logged-in operator read as "no auth" and
'   was sent looking for a token they did not need.
' BrokerProviders · filtered by what the harness DECLARES it can use. Unfiltered,
'   a claudecode run asked for cursor, google, openai and xai credentials too —
'   and sbx shows that list to the operator for approval, so an over-declared set
'   asks consent for reach the harness cannot exercise.
'
' ── EXISTENCE IS NOT VALIDITY, AND THE FILE LIES TWO WAYS ──────────────────
' PersistedLogin CLASSIFIES a credential file rather than stat-ing it. A dead
' credential is a file of exactly the same size as a live one, so presence alone
' let an expired login satisfy the guard that exists to stop a run the agent
' cannot complete — it reaches the login prompt, exits, and the sandbox stops with
' it, which reads as an infrastructure failure rather than "your login ran out".
'
' The stamps are read through POINTERS because ABSENT and ZERO mean opposite
' things: a missing field is a shape we do not understand, while an explicit 0 is
' a stamp deliberately cleared — exactly what a failed refresh leaves behind.
'
' The tokens are read for EMPTINESS only, never for value, and that is the second
' lie. Logging in on macOS moves the credential to the KEYCHAIN and rewrites the
' file with its tokens blanked and every stamp intact. Reading the stamps alone
' reported that as a login needing a refresh: the run announced itself
' authenticated, suppressed the env token that would have worked, and the agent
' died with nothing to send. LoginBlanked exists to say so out loud rather than
' letting it read as "no login".
'
' Three rules follow, and each is a run that failed without it:
'   * a blanked ACCESS token is not a login, however live the stamps look.
'   * a stale access token beside a LIVE refresh token IS still a login, and
'     needsRefresh. The agent renews at startup with no prompt; a CLEARED (0)
'     stamp lands here too, which is correct — it needs the same refresh, and
'     saying so is what explains the stall. A blanked REFRESH token is the
'     exception: the stamp describes a renewal that cannot happen.
'   * an UNRECOGNISED shape is reported USABLE, deliberately. This check exists to
'     catch a credential that is definitely dead; inferring "expired" from a shape
'     we cannot parse refuses runs that work, and a false refusal is worse than
'     the failure it prevents — the operator has no way to argue with it.
'
' ── WHAT THE RUN SAYS ABOUT ALL THIS, UP FRONT ─────────────────────────────
' Two lines exist because the alternative was silence at the moment it mattered.
'   needsRefresh · the agent renews the token itself, but its FIRST turn reports
'     "Login expired · Please run /login" while it does — which reads as a dead
'     credential to an operator, who then goes looking for an auth problem that
'     resolved itself a second later. One line up front costs nothing.
'   a token set and OMITTED · when a file login IS the credential and an auth var
'     is exported anyway, the var is suppressed and that is SAID. The MissingEnv
'     block only fires when auth is MISSING, so the case that actually misbills —
'     a token silently overriding a mounted subscription login — was the one
'     nothing reported.
'
' ── THE AUTH ROW ASKS HOW THE RUN IS BILLED ────────────────────────────────
' It used to list environment VARIABLE NAMES, which made it a spelling question.
' The operator had to already know that CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN is a plan and
' ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not, that CURSOR_API_KEY is a plan credential despite the
' name, and that OPENCODE_API_KEY is both plans of one gateway. Naming the two
' things actually being decided — `usage credits` and `subscription` — is what
' turns the row into a question about money rather than about spelling.
'
' Every def but cecli has both sides. cecli is an aider fork with no vendor of
' its own, so DeclaresSubscription is false and no row is drawn: a harness with
' one possible answer is not being asked anything.
'
' CLASSIFICATION IS THE MANIFEST'S JOB. A harness DECLARES its own credential in
' `env: secret: true`; everything else a provider registry entry offers is a
' usage key. That is why opencode's def had to declare OPENCODE_API_KEY at all —
' undeclared, it was not the plan, not forwardable under --credentials forward,
' and not a credential CredentialReachedAgent could see. The symptom the operator
' reported was the whole of that: "opencode seems unaware of the OPENCODE_API_KEY
' sub". internal/contract TestSubscriptionHarnesses pins the three declarations.
'
' ── THE OLD GATE COLLAPSED THE ROW ON THE ORDINARY HOST ────────────────────
' envAuthVars wanted EXACTLY ONE detected provider, else a manifest pin, else
' nil. A host holding OPENCODE_API_KEY beside ANTHROPIC_API_KEY detects two, pins
' neither, and got no row — so the one shape opencode is built for was the one
' shape that could not be answered. The gate is now availability per SIDE: usage
' credits when any spendable provider key is set, subscription when the declared
' credential is set or a login is on disk.
'
' A side stays ONE option however many keys back it. The keys over there are
' plural on purpose — the role bridges point different model slots at different
' vendors — so the question is which side authenticates, never which key.
'
' ── ONE HARNESS'S PLAN IS NOT ANOTHER'S PROVIDER KEY ───────────────────────
' ProviderKeyVars reads provider.AuthVarsFor(name, family), not AuthVars: a
' credential the registry marks as issued by some OTHER harness's plan is not
' spendable here. Without that, an opencode row offered CURSOR_API_KEY —
' set, real, and refused by every endpoint opencode can reach, because cursor's
' API answers cursor-agent and nothing else. Same for CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN,
' which authenticates Claude Code and nothing else.
' SPEC: _spec/internal/provider/provider-registry.puml
'
' ── THE CHOICE HAS TO BITE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
' AuthSuppressor now spans providers, in ONE direction each way:
'   `subscription` chosen → every provider key is withheld.
'   `usage credits` chosen → the harness's own declared credential is withheld.
' A run answered 'subscription' that still shipped ANTHROPIC_API_KEY billed
' whichever model slot happened to name anthropic — the same misbilling the
' single-provider rule above was written for, one level up. Vendor-pinned
' harnesses are EXEMPT: cursor has no far side, so a cross-provider rule there
' would be a rule with no case behind it.
'
' A login on disk still out-ranks an env token of the same provider, and an
' explicit `subscription` answer lands in that branch rather than resolving to
' the variable beside it. Letting the token win is what billed a subscription
' run as API usage.
'
' ── ONE CREDENTIAL CAN BUY BOTH SIDES ─────────────────────────────────────
' The row classifies the CREDENTIAL, and for claudecode that is right: the OAuth
' token is the plan, the API key is metered, and holding one tells you which. For
' a vendor GATEWAY it is not right, and opencode is the case that proves it.
'
' Zen (pay-as-you-go, `opencode/<model>`, opencode.ai/zen/v1, 102 models) and Go
' (the $10/mo subscription, `opencode-go/<model>`, opencode.ai/zen/go/v1, 35
' models) are one gateway on ONE ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" — models.dev lists that env
' for both. So an operator can answer `subscription`, hold exactly the right key,
' and still be billed per token because the model id names a Zen model. No
' credential check catches that; only the prefix does.
'
' provider.ModelBilling reads the RAW prefix, before providerAliases folds
' opencode-go into opencode. That fold is correct for ROUTING — one host, one
' key, one broker route — and destroys the only thing the two prefixes exist to
' distinguish, so this question has to be asked before it happens.
'
' Cursor is the same shape with less visibility: one ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" spends the
' plan's included usage and then usage-based overage, and the split happens on
' Cursor's side where proveo cannot see it at all. So its models get no billing
' verdict and the row says outright that it cannot decide.
'
' TWO CONSEQUENCES. A gateway credential BACKS BOTH OPTIONS rather than being
' filed under one — opencode with only its own key still offers both. And the
' hint carries a caveat instead of the usual "choosing this withholds the other",
' because that clause would be a promise proveo cannot keep. Go even falls back
' to the Zen balance once its limits are spent when 'Use balance' is on, so a
' correct opencode-go/ id can become metered mid-run.
'
' Roles.BillingClashes warns where the operator's own two answers disagree. It
' warns and never blocks: proveo cannot promise a billing side, only point at
' the contradiction.
'
' ── "USAGE CREDITS" MEANS TWO THINGS, AND CURSOR SEPARATES THEM ────────────
' Bring-your-own-key is one; metered spend on the vendor's own gateway is the
' other. Cursor has the second and not the first, so gating its usage option was
' wrong twice over — it CAN be billed metered, and an operator whose
' ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" does nothing there still needs told why. Both sides are
' offered; VendorPinnedWhy stays the explanation for the BYOK half alone.
'
' ── MODEL RESOLUTION HAS THREE TIERS, AND FEASIBILITY GATES EACH ──────────
'   1. the remembered answer  this agent's own saved choice — the most specific
'                             and most recent thing the operator said
'   2. the .env / shell value ambient, and written for some other run
'   3. the plan default       planFallback, ordered, judgement written down
'
' The order used to be the REVERSE of 1 and 2. MergeRoles let an ARCHITECT_MODEL
' exported once in a shell rc silently override the answer the operator had just
' given this agent at the prompt — every run, with nothing said. Nothing pinned
' the order, which is how it stayed that way; TestMergeRolesPrefersTheRemembered-
' Answer is the pin.
'
' Feasibility gates EVERY tier, not just one. A tier naming a provider this run
' has no credential for, or one its auth answer withholds, is skipped and the
' next tried — .env is tier 2, not a tiebreak, so an unusable remembered choice
' falls THROUGH it rather than past it. Every skip is reported with which tier
' and why: a silent substitution is worse than the warning it replaces, and the
' operator is owed the reason their typed value was not used.
'
' ── FEASIBILITY IS NOT A TTY QUESTION ─────────────────────────────────────
' The two halves of resolution answer to different things. "This provider has no
' credential" is objective and applies to a headless run exactly as it does to a
' watched one — launching on a model that cannot be called, then warning to a log
' nobody reads until the job fails, is the worst of both. "This is the wrong
' billing side" is a judgement against an answer, and with no answer there is no
' wrong side.
'
' So feasibility always applies and the billing side only when someone was asked.
' AnsweredBilling returns BillUnknown for an unanswered run and PlanFallback
' resolves that through the plan list — the only side a gateway harness populates
' — WITHOUT claiming the operator chose it.
'
' ── AND TIER 3 MUST NOT ASSUME AN ENTITLEMENT ─────────────────────────────
' The first version of it did, and the mistake is instructive: it used the very
' finding this file is built on to justify contradicting it.
'
' Holding OPENCODE_API_KEY does not say which PLAN it entitles — Zen and Go
' share the variable — so a fallback of `opencode-go/muse-spark-1.3-contributor`
' asserts a subscription proveo cannot see.
'
' And holding Go turned out not to be sufficient either. An operator who DID
' hold Go had that id rejected as //"Agent build's configured model … is not
' valid"//, after which opencode silently fell through to **Whisper Large V3
' Turbo** on Groq: a 2024 speech-to-text model, driving a coding agent, with no
' error they could act on. The substitution had replaced a KNOWN problem
' (anthropic withheld, stated plainly in three warnings) with an unknown one,
' which is strictly worse — the operator lost the diagnosis along with the run.
'
' THAT CAUSE IS STILL UNKNOWN. The same id resolves correctly headlessly:
' through this image's entrypoint, with the seeded config's
' `{env:OPENCODE_BUILD_MODEL}` placeholder, with the env var alone, and with
' the broker sentinel in place — `providerID=opencode-go` every time, failing
' only on the key. The unreproduced difference is the TUI. Ruled out along the
' way: entitlement, the provider block `/connect` would write, the sentinel,
' the persisted home config, and the bridge's `normalize` transform (which
' returns early on any id containing a slash).
'
' Which is the argument for the `-free` tier rather than a footnote to it: it
' depends on neither the entitlement nor on whatever went wrong there. It is
' the model that RUNS, not the best one, and it says so — the note names it as
' the free tier and points at naming an `opencode-go/` or `opencode/` model to
' choose properly. A Go subscriber gets their plan through tier 1 or 2, where
' they asked for it.
'
' scripts/rank-plan-fallbacks.py refuses `opencode-go` as a source outright and
' ranks only zero-cost ids. TestFallbacksNeverAssumeAnEntitlement fails the
' build on a plan-gated prefix or a priced id.
'
' ── TIER 3 IS A LIST BECAUSE A PIN ROTS SILENTLY ──────────────────────────
' models.dev is opencode's own registry and the source proveo already syncs. It
' carries release_date, last_updated, cost, context and capabilities for all 35
' Go models. What it does NOT carry is an opinion: there is no 'recommended'
' field, every Go model reports tool_call:true so capability cannot
' discriminate, and deriving 'newest' picks `omen-alpha` — with `hy4-preview`
' and `ox-alpha-free` close behind. A registry says what exists; it cannot say
' what is safe to point an agent at.
'
' So the judgement stays in the tree, ordered and reviewable, and DEGRADES:
' muse-spark-1.3-contributor (2026-09-02, 1.05M ctx, $0.10/M in) then glm-5.3
' (2026-08-14, 1M ctx, flagship). PlanFallback returns the first entry the
' registry still resolves; TestPlanFallbacksAreRealModels fails the build when
' every entry has gone stale — the moment a human should look again.
'
' ── THE ROLE VARS ARE A PREFERENCE, NOT A MANDATE ─────────────────────────
' Answering `subscription` has to LAND the operator on their subscription, and
' auth is only half of that. opencode registers Zen AND Go from one
' OPENCODE_API_KEY with no /connect — verified against the built image, where
' `opencode auth list` shows both plans and auth.json holds nothing. But the
' bridge default for OPENCODE_MODEL is anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5, so a Go
' subscriber holding only that one key lands on a provider they have no key
' for. They skip /connect and hit /models instead; the in-session step moved
' rather than went away.
'
' So a role model that cannot authenticate YIELDS. A value carried in a shell
' rc or a project .env was written for some other run, and honouring it when
' the credential behind it is absent or withheld produces a session that cannot
' make a single model call — then asks the operator to fix it from inside the
' broken run. Roles.Feasible substitutes the plan's own model for exactly those
' roles and SAYS which, per role, with the reason: withheld by the answer, or
' no credential at all. Anything runnable is left exactly as written.
'
' The fallback is provisional and will go stale: Go rotates its lineup (35
' models when this was written), so TestPlanFallbacksAreRealModels checks the id
' still resolves through the registry AND still lands on the side it claims.
' A stale id there is a run that dies on an unknown model; the test makes it a
' build failure instead.
'
' ── AND THE CACHE HAS TO GIVE BACK WHAT IT WAS GIVEN ──────────────────────
' Storing that fallback exposed an older bug. Roles.Canonical ran every value
' through normalizeIntent, which turns "." into "-" — right for an INTENT a
' human typed ("Kimi K3" -> "kimi-k3"), fatal for an ADDRESS. Through the
' choice cache, `opencode-go/glm-5.3` came back `opencode-go/glm-5-3`: a model
' that does not exist, handed to the agent on the SECOND run of anyone who let
' the prompt remember their answer.
'
' It survived because anthropic ids carry no dots. The defaults could not trip
' it and only a Zen or Go model would — which is to say, only once this row
' started pointing people at one. A provider-qualified id is now kept verbatim
' and a bare string is still normalized: the slash is the same line the registry
' already draws between an address it can route and a string it has to guess at.
'
' ── THE ANSWER HAS TO REACH THE BROKER ────────────────────────────────────
' Suppression stopped at the container's environment. The broker injects
' on-route at the egress hop BY DESIGN, so withholding a key from the agent
' while the proxy still attaches it to that provider's own requests left the
' answer true of the environment and false of the wire.
'
' Observed in run proveo-1788665157-82784: `auth var subscription` beside
' `brokered anthropic,cursor,openai,xai,google,opencode` — six providers on the
' wire for a run that named one side, with every model role on anthropic. The
' operator's protection was illusory and nothing said so.
'
' WithheldProviders drops a provider only when EVERY credential it could have
' used is suppressed; one surviving variable keeps the route. Detected stays
' honest about what the host holds — only what is INJECTED narrows.
'
' UsableProviders is the neighbouring fix: an opencode run on a developer's
' machine detected cursor and brokered it, so the proxy stood ready to attach
' ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" to requests opencode cannot make and cursor's API would
' refuse. A route for a credential this harness may not send is reach it cannot
' use — and on sbx that list is shown to the operator for approval.
'
' ── THE HINT NAMES THE VARIABLES AND THE FILE ──────────────────────────────
' AuthBacking is what an option is MADE of on THIS host. Two bare nouns leave the
' operator unable to tell whether "subscription" means the token in their shell
' rc, the key in the project .env, or the login sitting in the proveo home — and
' those are three different credentials with three different bills. So the hint
' carries the variable names, the .env path when a key actually lives in one
' (saying "host env" over a .env value sends them to the wrong file), and the
' login file path when a login is what will be spent.
'
' AuthWhyUnavailable is the other half. An option gated with no reason is a dead
' end, and this row gates one or two of three on an ordinary host.
'
' ── "NO CREDENTIAL" IS A QUESTION ABOUT THE RUN, NOT THE VARIABLE ──────────
' HasUsableAuth is the guard on every path that says a harness has nothing to
' authenticate with. Reading only the manifest's declared env asked "is the PLAN
' credential missing", which is a different question for a harness that also
' takes provider keys: opencode backed by ANTHROPIC_API_KEY HAS a credential.
' Two paths were answering the wrong one — SandboxAuthRefusal, which refuses the
' run outright on the sbx backend, and NeedsSandboxLogin, which tells the
' operator to go and log in. Both now yield to a usable provider key.
'
' SandboxAuthRefusal also speaks for the harness it refused. The refusal used to
' be a hardcoded string: whatever harness hit it was told to run `claude
' setup-token` and export CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, so a cursor run with no
' CURSOR_API_KEY was handed claudecode's instructions. It now reads
' SubscriptionAuthHints for that harness, and offers the "export a provider key
' instead" line only where the harness can actually use one.
'
' ── THE ONE WRITER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' WriteBrokerEnv is the only function here that touches disk, and it keeps 0600.
' WarnMountedSecrets takes its writer as an argument rather than reaching for a
' package global, so a caller's test can capture what it says instead of watching
' stderr.
title internal/credentials — decisions as pure functions

participant "run.resolveCredentials" as RUN <<app>>
participant "lookup\nenv-then-file" as LK <<data>>
participant "credentials\n(pure)" as CR <<app>>
participant "inject/broker.env\n0600" as F <<data>>

RUN ARROW_MAIN CR : manifest + lookup + homeRoot
LK ARROW_QUEUE CR : the ONLY credential read
CR ARROW_MAIN RUN : detected · brokered · suppressed · hints
CR ARROW_OPTIONAL F : WriteBrokerEnv — the one writer

note over CR
  **No env reads of its own.** Everything arrives
  as an argument, which is why the package moved
  out of main.go with no golden change and why
  each rule can be tested in isolation.
end note

note over CR
  **Existence is not validity.** A dead
  credential file is exactly the size of a live
  one, so stat-ing it let an expired login pass
  the guard meant to stop a run the agent
  cannot finish.
end note

note over CR
  **A false refusal is worse than the failure.**
  An unrecognised shape is reported USABLE:
  inferring 'expired' from bytes we cannot parse
  blocks runs that work, and the operator has no
  way to argue with it.
end note

note over LK
  **Tokens are read for EMPTINESS, never value.**
  macOS moves the credential to the Keychain and
  blanks the file's tokens with every stamp
  intact — read as a login needing a refresh, it
  suppressed the env token that did work.
end note

note over RUN
  **A renewal is announced up front.** The agent
  renews the token itself, but its first turn
  prints 'Login expired' while it does — which
  sends the operator after a problem that had
  already resolved itself.
end note

note over CR
  **The row asks how the run is BILLED**, not
  which variable to spell: usage credits, or the
  plan the harness's vendor issues. cecli
  declares no plan and is asked nothing.
end note

note over CR
  **The old gate collapsed the row.** It wanted
  exactly one detected provider, so
  ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" next to an anthropic key
  produced no row at all.
end note

note over CR
  **The choice bites both ways.** The plan
  withholds every provider key; usage withholds
  the harness's own. A run answered 'subscription'
  that still shipped a key billed per token.
end note

note over LK
  **One harness's plan is not another's key.**
  ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" is set, real, and refused by
  every endpoint opencode reaches — so the
  registry marks who issues a credential.
end note

note over RUN
  **'No credential' is about the RUN.** Reading
  the declared env asked whether the PLAN key was
  missing — so opencode on ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY""
  was refused, over a key it never needed.
end note

note over RUN
  **A refusal speaks for what it refused.** It was
  one hardcoded string: a cursor run with no key
  was told to run ""claude setup-token"".
end note

note over CR
  **One credential can buy BOTH sides.** Zen
  (""opencode/<m>"", metered) and Go
  (""opencode-go/<m>"", the plan) are one gateway
  on one key — the MODEL decides, not the row.
end note

note over CR
  So a gateway key backs both options, and the
  hint carries a caveat rather than "choosing
  this withholds the other" — a promise proveo
  cannot keep when the vendor decides.
end note

note over LK
  **Cursor is the same shape, less visible.** One
  key spends the plan's usage then overage, split
  on Cursor's side. Its models get no verdict;
  the row says it cannot decide.
end note

note over CR
  **A fallback must not assume an entitlement.**
  ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" does not say which plan it
  buys, so naming a Go model guessed one.
end note

note over CR
  An operator who DID hold Go still saw it called
  invalid, then **Whisper Large V3 Turbo** — a
  2024 speech model driving a coding agent.
end note

note over CR
  That cause is unknown: the id resolves fine
  headlessly, sentinel included. Only the TUI
  path is unreproduced — which is why the
  fallback must not depend on it.
end note

note over CR
  So tier 3 names only the ""-free"" tier, served
  to any key. The model that RUNS, not the best
  one — and the note says which it is.
end note

note over RUN
  **A role model that cannot authenticate
  YIELDS.** The operator's ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"" is
  a preference written for some other run, not a
  mandate for this one.
end note

note over RUN
  Answering 'subscription' on a Go-only host used
  to land on ""anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5"" — the
  bridge default — so /connect was skipped and
  /models was hit instead.
end note

note over CR
  **The cache has to give back what it was
  given.** ""Canonical"" normalized every value,
  so ""opencode-go/glm-5.3"" returned as
  ""glm-5-3"" — a model that does not exist.
end note

note over CR
  It survived because anthropic ids carry no
  dots. Only a Zen or Go model trips it, which is
  to say only once this row started pointing
  people at one.
end note

note over RUN
  **The answer has to reach the BROKER.** It
  injects on-route by design, so withholding a
  key from the agent while the proxy attached it
  anyway made the answer true only of the env.
end note

note over RUN
  Observed: ""auth var subscription"" beside six
  brokered providers, every role on anthropic.
  The protection was illusory and silent.
end note

note over RUN
  **A set-but-omitted token is SAID.** The
  missing-env block only fires when auth is
  MISSING, so a token silently overriding a
  mounted subscription login was the one case
  that misbills and reported nothing.
end note
@enduml
